## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a pragmatic, scalable program that links people, process, and outcomes. Evaluates how to allocate limited time for long‑term health without killing velocity, and how to guard against gaming.

## Key Concepts

- Team health budgeting and non-delivery work
- Cross‑team governance in multi-time‑zone setups
- ROI metrics: defect density, cycle time, retention
- Anti-gaming controls and sunset reviews

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple scoring for health proposals
function scoreProposal(p) {
  const impact = p.impact * 0.4;
  const risk = (1 - p.timeliness) * 0.3;
  const effort = 1 / (p.effort + 0.01);
  return impact + risk + effort;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle proposals that seem high ROI but strain critical squads?
- What metrics would you track to ensure long-term health gains persist beyond one quarter?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests ability to design governance for cross-team experimentation, balancing speed with reliability and data integrity.

## Key Concepts
- KPI ownership and charter
- Preflight power analysis and data-quality gates
- Guardrails: latency, error rate, quotas
- Feature flags, canary and cohort rollouts
- Artifacts: experiment charter, data map, rollback plan

## Code Example
```javascript
function shouldRollout(experiment, metrics) {
  const {power, mde, dataQuality} = metrics;
  if (!experiment.flagsEnabled) return false;
  if (power < experiment.requiredPower) return false;
  if (!dataQuality.ok) return false;
  if (metrics.latency > experiment.maxLatency) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would thresholds vary by product area?
- How would you measure guardrail impact on velocity and reliability?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to translate a performance problem into a concrete, actionable plan with beginner-level scope.

## Key Concepts

- Telemetry-driven optimization
- Safe experiments with feature flags
- Incident response basics
- Stakeholder communication

## Code Example

```javascript
// Tiny cache helper for hot paths
const cache = new Map();
async function getCached(key, fetch){
  if (cache.has(key)) return cache.get(key);
  const val = await fetch();
  cache.set(key, val);
  return val;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure success if latency improvements reduce throughput?
- What would you change if a third-party dependency introduces higher error rates after caching is added?

## Why This Is Asked
Gauges ability to operationalize privacy across multiple squads and regions while preserving velocity, balancing governance with execution.

## Key Concepts
- Privacy-by-design and DPIA governance
- Data minimization and consent management
- CI gates and artifact templates
- Region-specific privacy budgets and rituals

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo CI gate: enforce privacy checks before merge
function privacyGate(feature) {
  if (!feature.hasDPIA || !feature.consentSchemaValid) {
    throw new Error('Privacy gate failed');
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure fatigue or compliance drift across regions?
- What would you automate to keep DPIA upkeep lightweight for fast-moving features?
